In this lesson, we'll look at how to prove two triangles are exactly the same shape and size, which is what we call congruent. Two triangles are congruent when you could lift one up and place it perfectly on top of the other. To show that, you don't have to check all three sides and all three angles. You just need one of four shortcut tests. The first is SSS, meaning all three pairs of sides are equal. The second is SAS, where two sides are equal and the angle squished between them, the included angle, is also equal. The third is AAS, where two angles are equal and any one non-included side matches. The fourth is RHS, which only works for right-angled triangles and needs the right angle, the hypotenuse, and one other side. Once you've proved the triangles congruent using one of these tests, every matching side and matching angle is equal too — that's the rule called corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent. When you tackle a problem, read what's given and pick the test whose three pieces match. If you've only got two equal sides, remember you almost always need the included angle for SAS, or the angles at the ends for AAS. Now let's see it in action.